I met my best friend, @PERSON1, in third grade. Best friends stay with you through the easy moments and the hard times. @PERSON1 and I learned from each other and shared experiences, no matter the distance. Laughter between two best friends bonds them, creating shared moments cherished for life. @PERSON1 and I have grown up together, supporting each other's needs, from helping a friend learning how to jump rope at school, to dealing with school-girl drama, crushes, boyfriends, heartbreaks, weddings, and funerals. We offered advice and brought hot chocolate, and, most importantly, learned to laugh at our mistakes and move on. 

@PERSON1 is one of those friends that can make you smile just by seeing or hearing them. We have shared many memories together, from lying in the driveway and watching the stars at night, to cracking up while working backstage for the local drama club. Her perfect imitations of anyone always make me laugh, even in the worst of moods. While I unintentionally cracked her up by saying or doing something ridiculous, which shows my true blond side. We have laughed over lines from our favorite movies and jotted down the funniest moments. 

One of the funniest times we ever spent together was when we worked backstage during a production by our local theater club. Everything from building sets to mistakes on stage made us laugh. Stubbing my toes while rolling out sets was followed by @PERSON1 frantically tugging on a rope that kept getting caught in the pulley. We both enjoyed watching the audience, director, and actors all at the same time. We giggled silently with each other, watching the actors' mistakes and the director's horrified face. Even mentioning any word or line from that play makes us both start cracking up and reminiscing over the entire weekend. 

@PERSON1 and I can make each other laugh without even explaining what we meant, because we have shared so many experiences. Just one key expression or a twinkle in our eyes can crack the other up. Nonverbal messages shared between best friends can bring up subjects never spoken about and create laughter. We understand each other's silent codes, knowing that only the intended recipient can figure them out. Bonding through laughter is what makes true best friends close, leaving them with cherished memories to look back on.

Human beings are, to put it plainly, strange creatures. We have a fundamental need to be liked by others, yet even if we are liked, we sometimes still dislike ourselves. We use relationships with others to gain a sense of understanding about ourselves, which is a risky gamble. We give others the power to either build us up or break us down. As youngsters, our main goal is to find people who like us, but this desire may extend into adulthood. Two key traits to look for in people who can build us up are trust and kindness. While kindness is a great way to make friends, it may not be the fastest approach. The fastest way to know a friend is to look for someone who makes you laugh. Laughter is a golden rule in the book of life, if there is such a thing. It was through laughter that I improved my experience in @CAPS2 class.

@CAPS2 class was extremely stressful for me. Every day, we had to do either a speaking assignment or paperwork, and the class moved at a snail's pace due to constant interruptions. However, there was one day when everything changed. We had an assignment to create a script using the vocabulary we learned from a booklet. We had a whole class period to complete it, and then we went home to do other work. The next day, the teacher lined up strange props, and we found out we were going to have the audience act out our script while we read it out loud. My group was the first to present, and we chose our volunteers and actors. As we sat down ready to read the script, uncertain about what the actors would do, we were surprised to find that everyone was giggling by the end of the first sentence. As we moved through the other plays, the laughter grew louder. More and more people volunteered to perform until it was difficult to choose actors. Even I, who had previously been an unhappy classroom snob, was enjoying myself. The plays were hilarious, and some of them made me laugh so hard that my sides ached, tears lined my lids, and it was almost impossible to breathe. This project was the best one I had ever participated in.

Moments like these are when we build strong unity in the classroom. We can enjoy the people around us and ourselves. I was so used to being angry at my classmates that I was taken by surprise on that one day. I enjoyed their plays so much that I started to see them as a group of entertaining students instead of the annoyance I had previously believed them to be. They are no longer strangers in the hallway; now they are family. It's amazing how something as simple as laughter can change one's perspective on others and oneself.

@ORGANIZATION1 brings joy to my life through the power of laughter. I have always believed that laughter is a crucial component to any relationship, and that was made evident during a memorable experience with my cousin @CAPS1. One day, @CAPS1's father, @PERSON1, gave us a Monster Energy drink to share. While we were not supposed to have any, we couldn't resist the temptation, especially since we were headed to @ORGANIZATION2 in @LOCATION1. However, after ten minutes, @CAPS1, who only took a few sips, was acting crazy while I, who had consumed the most, felt calm. @CAPS1 was running down aisles, tripping over her own feet, and almost tipping our shopping cart with me in it. Despite there being no evident reason for it, @CAPS1's tendency to burst into laughter hysterically is typical for her. Eventually, Wendy, @PERSON1's wife and my second cousin, became hungry, so we went to a cafe at @ORGANIZATION2 to grab some pizza. However, while my mother called me, I was trying to retrieve my phone from my pocket while holding my pizza plate. As my grip slipped, I handed the plate to @CAPS1, who was simultaneously holding her own food. After feeling the bottom of the plate, @CAPS1 exclaimed that it was hot, causing her to pass it off to @PERSON1, who wasn't paying attention and didn't have a good grip on it, resulting in my pizza hitting the floor, with the cheese side downwards. This embarrassing moment caused a few onlookers to stare at us.      

Recognizing his mistake, @PERSON1 told @CAPS1 to go get some churros. However, while @CAPS1 was away, we collectively realized our error, fearing that we may have jinxed it by calling her a klutz. Nevertheless, we were surprised when @CAPS1 returned with the churros, without dropping them. However, our surprise was short-lived as one of the churros fell off the table once @CAPS1 placed them down. This act of clumsiness caused us to laugh uncontrollably, so much so that we were gasping for air. Despite @CAPS1 feeling embarrassed, she knew it was funny, but she didn't want to join in on the laughter. In such situations, I remind myself of one thing: If you can't laugh at yourself or be willing to do so, then you are not truly having fun. Laughing is crucial, especially when you need to be able to laugh at yourself. Even when you don't feel like it, it is important to learn how to have fun, even when you have made a silly mistake that you think everyone noticed. Sometimes, it is our job to encourage others by patting them on the back, telling them that everybody makes mistakes, and that nobody is perfect. We tell them to laugh it off, as it serves as a reminder that life isn't perfect, and that the ability to laugh at ourselves is important.

Laughter is significant because the more you laugh, the better you feel, and subsequently, the more fun you experience. Laughter is the perfect remedy for the little things that happen in life, and it helps in healing the wounds that life sometimes inflicts upon us.
